  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-(OXFAM)-LEAP PHASE II</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project contributes to and is aligned to the UN Women &#x201c;Advancing Women&#x2019;s Leadership, Empowerment and Resilience in Uganda&#x2019;s Refugee Response: Phase II&#x201d; and directly contributes to Outcome 3. The project intends to achieve the following results:

Goal: Positive coping mechanisms and sustainable economic activities are promoted for refugee and host community women and girls.

Outcome 1.0:  Refugee and host community women have increased access to sustainable non-agricultural livelihood opportunities.

Output 1.1:    Establishment and roll out of savings and lending groups to (105)   3,150 women including digitalisation of savings and linkages to financial institutions such as banks. (10 percent PWDs) -Implemented by OXFAM and URDMC

The 105 women&apos;s groups, each with 30 members, engaged in income-generating activities of the Village Savings and Loan Association methodology through engagement with the ENSIBUUKO company for digital VSLA. This will help in the profiling of VSLA and Framers groups, financial linkages, a digital marketplace for farmers, and e-learning through an Android-based smart phone application. These groups will also receive support in the form of tabs or smartphones to aid their saving and financial literacy training and groupdynamics training. Oxfam and its partners will work with the district commercial officer, district community development officer, and office of the prime minister to monitor the groups,minimise related risk, and ensure sustainability once they are matureand have been saving for at least a year.

Output 1.2: Support75 women groups comprising of 2,250 women and youth to establish and manage small and enterprises, support with business and financial skills training including capitalization of businessesand linkages to value addition chains for refugee women through increasingproduction, and support to establish cottage industries and linkages to market opportunities.  Implemented by OXFAM</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib is one of the 17 national affiliates of the Oxfam International Confederation. It started life in 1956 as the Netherlands Organisation for International Assistance (Novib), and joined forces with Oxfam in 1994.  Working together in confederation, the Oxfam affiliates can speak with one clear and compelling voice on issues of global concern: climate change, health and education forall, agriculture, trade, and conflicts and emergencies.  Oxfam established this country office to support local organizations and projects.  Through this local office Oxfam Novib directly contributes towards this project&#x92;s implementation.</narrative>
